Writing to a file in fopen(“example.txt or specified path”, “w/r/…”), a new file can be used to write to (example.txt) or a specified path can be inputted as well.
fprintf(pFile, “Age: %d”, age): Best for mixed data types, variable, and formatted text.
fputs(“Hello World”, pFile): Best for plain strings or whole lines of text without variables.
fputc(‘A’, pFile): Best for single characters or writing file data byte-by-byte.


#include <stdio.h>


int main() {


    FILE *pFile = fopen("output.txt", "w");


    char text[] = "Hello World!";


    if (pFile == NULL) {
        printf("Error opening file");
        return 1; // Exit code - Error
    }


    fprintf(pFile, "%s", text);


    printf("File was written successfully\n");


    fclose(pFile);


    return 0;
}










Reading a file needs a buffer to temporarily hold the data to be read and use 1024 as a size to hold the info (could use higher if needed). 
The while() reads the file until it reaches NULL and that means that the data is all read.
fgets(buffer, size, pFile): Best for text files. Reads line-by-line safely by enforcing size limits.
fscanf(pFile, "%s %d", str, &num): Best for formatted data. Parses spaces, strings, and numeric values seamlessly.
fgetc(pFile): Best for character parsing. Reads the file strictly one character at a time.
fread(buffer, size, count, pFile): Best for binary structures. Reads raw blocks of raw memory efficiently

#include <stdio.h>


int main() {


    FILE *pFile = fopen("input.txt", "r");


    char buffer[1024] = {0};


    if (pFile == NULL) {
        printf("Could not open file");
        return 1; // Exit Error
    }


    while(fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), pFile) != NULL) {
        printf("%s", buffer);
    }


    fclose(pFile);


    return 0;
}


